<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>4. Virtual DOM Diffing</=
strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The reconciliation algorithm per=
forms a <strong>tree diffing</strong> process that operates on three key pr=
inciples:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Same-level comparison o=
nly</strong>: React never compares nodes across different tree levels</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Element type and key id=
entity</strong>: Elements with different types or keys are considered compl=
etely different</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Recursive descent</stro=
ng>: The diff process recursively updates child elements</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>5. Commit Phase</strong>=
</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Finally, the changes are applied=
 to the actual DOM in the commit phase, where React batches DOM mutations f=
or optimal performance.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ex=
cellent overview! Now let's dig deeper. You mentioned that React only compa=
res nodes at the same level. This is a crucial optimization that makes Reac=
t's diff algorithm O(n) instead of O(n=C2=B3). Can you explain why this tra=
de-off was made and what are the implications for developers?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: That=
's a brilliant question that touches on one of React's most important archi=
tectural decisions. The <strong>O(n) vs O(n=C2=B3) trade-off</strong> is fu=
ndamental to React's performance characteristics.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Why O(n=C2=B3) is proble=
matic:</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Traditional tree diffing algorit=
hms have O(n=C2=B3) complexity because they need to:</p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Find the minimum number of oper=
ations to transform one tree into another</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Consider all possible node move=
ments across different levels</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Evaluate every possible subtree=
 transformation</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">For a tree with 1000 nodes, this=
 means potentially 1 billion operations - completely impractical for real-t=
ime UI updates.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>React's O(n) approach:</=
strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">React made several <strong>strat=
egic assumptions</strong> based on real-world UI patterns:</p>
